'''wall<sup>1</sup>''' (wâl), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''walle'', ''wallen'', wall, surround with walls.] '''1.''' To inclose with a wall or as with a wall; furnish with walls: as, to ''wall'' a city.
{{smaller block|<poem>Certes the Kyng of Thebes, Amphioun,
That with his syngyng ''walled'' that citee.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Manciple’s Tale, l. 13.}}
This flesh which ''walls'' about our life.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., iii. 2. 167.}}}}
'''2.''' To defend by walls; fortify.
{{smaller block|<poem>The terror of his name that ''walls'' us in
From danger.</poem>
{{right|''Denham.''}}}}
'''3.''' To obstruct or hinder as by a wall.
{{smaller block|<poem>On either hand thee there are squadrons pitch’d,
To ''wall'' thee from the liberty of flight.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., iv. 2. 24.}}}}
'''4.''' To fill up with a wall.
{{smaller block|The ascent [to the mosque of Sultan Hassan] was by several steps, which are broken down, and the door ''wall’d'' up.
{{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, I. 31.}}}}
'''5.''' In ''Eng. university slang'', same as ''gate''.
{{smaller block|To gate or ''wall'' a refractory student.
{{right|''Macmillan’s Mag.'', II. 222.}}}}

