'''wallaba''' (wol′a-bä), ''n.'' [Guiana name (?).] See ''Eperua.''

'''wallaby''' (wol′a-bi), ''n.'' [Also ''wallabee, whallabee;'' from an Australian name.] A general native name of the smaller kangaroos of Australia, especially those of the genera ''Halmaturus'' and ''Petrogale;'' a rock-kangaroo.

{{smaller block|“What does your lordship suppose a ''wallaby'' to be?” “Why, a half-caste, of course.” “A ''wallaby'', my lord, is a dwarf kangaroo.” {{right|''Contemporary Rev.'', LIII. 3.}}}}

{{smaller|'''On the wallaby, on the wallaby track,''' out of work; in search of a job: the wallaby being proverbially shy and elusive. [Slang, Australia.]—'''Wallaby acacia''' or '''wattle,''' an Australian shrub, ''Acacia rigens'', having in place of leaves linear phyllodia 2 or 3 inches long.—'''Wallaby-bush,''' an Australian evergreen shrub, ''Beyeria viscosa,'' of the ''Euphorbiaceæ;'' also, other species of the genus.—'''Wallaby-grass,''' ''Danthonia penicillata'' of Australia.}}

'''Wallace’s line.''' See ''line''<sup>2</sup>.

